**Goal** — Return a normal not-started result for a valid feature with no artifacts, with only nearby feature-name suggestions.

**Motivation** — #2927: `nax-workflow__state` for a feature that has no spec yet returns `unknown feature <name>; known: <20 names>, +N more` as an error, so every new-feature conversation starts with a failed tool call and a list of unrelated names.

**Approach** — `nax-workflow__state` for a valid feature name with no feature directory and no legacy spec returns a non-error JSON result `{ name, stage: "not-started", spec: null, prd: null, similar }`, where `similar` holds at most three close matches from the known feature directories. The long known-feature list is no longer returned.

The not-started result is a different shape from `FeatureState` on purpose: `stage: "not-started"` is the field that tells the agent the feature has no artifacts yet, and `spec`/`prd` are null as in `FeatureState`.

Baseline: when neither the feature directory nor `.nax/specs/<feature>.md` exists, returns `failure(`unknown feature ${feature}; known: ${available}${suffix}`)`. Target: returns a non-error `NotStartedFeature` JSON result (see below). Retain repo resolution, feature validation, directory filtering through both validateFeatureName and featureDir, legacy spec support and existing feature-state/list readers.

**Scope** — In: absent-feature branch in workflow-provider.ts, pure similarity helper, replacement of old unknown-feature assertions. Out: general-agent dispatch (US-002), workflow options and prompt text (US-007), changing FeatureState or list shapes.

**Interface** —
```typescript
// packages/nax/src/chat/workflow/similar-features.ts
export const MAX_SIMILAR_FEATURES = 3;
/** Known names within Levenshtein distance max(2, floor(name.length / 4)) of `name` (case-sensitive, over the names as given), closest first, ties by name; at most MAX_SIMILAR_FEATURES. */
export function similarFeatureNames(name: string, known: readonly string[]): string[];

// packages/nax/src/chat/workflow/workflow-provider.ts — the not-started result body
interface NotStartedFeature {
  readonly name: string;
  readonly stage: "not-started";
  readonly spec: null;
  readonly prd: null;
  readonly similar: readonly string[];
}
```

Update packages/nax/test/unit/chat/workflow/workflow-provider.test.ts: the tests "AC-11 unknown feature without feature tree", "does not suggest directories rejected by the feature path validator", "AC-15 sorts known directory names" and "AC-23 bounds valid known directories to 20" assert the old error. Replace them with the new invariants: the result is not an error, `stage` is `not-started`, and `similar` lists only valid close matches (never a directory the feature-name validator rejects).

**Goal** — Tell a nested native subagent its actual mapped allowed tools and how to return work its own tools cannot finish.

**Motivation** — The chat then picked the read-only `planner`, which could not write the spec and asked the human, twice, to enable write tools, although the parent chat had them.

**Approach** — A nested native subagent whose agent declares `tools` receives a tools section in its instructions: the tools it has in this run (plus the always-on scratchpad tools), and the fact that a step those tools cannot do goes in its final answer (what is left and where its draft is) for the calling agent, rather than in a question to the person.

`SubagentInstructionsInput` gains `readonly allowedTools?: readonly string[]`; when defined, a tools section is placed directly before the agent prompt. Its first sentence is `Your tools in this run: <allowedTools joined by ", ">, plus the always-on scratchpad tools.` (entries, including patterns such as `nax-skills__*`, rendered as given), or `Your tools in this run: only the always-on scratchpad tools.` for `[]`. Its second sentence is `The agent that called you has its own tools; a step your tools cannot do goes in your final answer, with what is left and the path of any draft, rather than in a question to the person.`

`mapSubagentTools(tools)` returns `{ allowedTools?: string[]; dropped }`; `allowedTools` is undefined when the agent declares no `tools`. `runNested` already passes `allowedTools` to `runSubagent`; US-003 threads it from `subagentSessionOptions` into `buildSubagentInstructions`; its seam AC enters at `runSubagent` and observes `_subagentSessionDeps.createAgentSession`.

All prompt text lives in `packages/nax/src/prompts/builders/` (project rule). New instruction text states facts the agent can use; it adds no new prohibitions. `buildSubagentInstructions` output is unchanged when `allowedTools` is undefined, which is every existing test's input. Preserve permission paragraph, rules index, memory and agent prompt order.

**Scope** — In: agents-builder.ts optional native tools section and subagent-session.ts input threading, unit and createAgentSession seam regressions. Out: general dispatch and index changes (US-002), config and workflow instruction choices (US-005/US-007), changing tool mapping, permission enforcement or ACP prompts. Classifying a subagent's question as a tooling blocker and rerouting it; the subagent is told its tools instead.

**Interface** —
```typescript
interface SubagentInstructionsInput {
  readonly agent: Pick<AgentResource, "prompt">;
  readonly mode: "full" | "guarded";
  readonly rulesIndex: readonly RulesIndexEntry[];
  readonly memoryIndex?: string;
  readonly allowedTools?: readonly string[];
}
```

**Goal** — Add validated layered workflow settings without changing the default configuration when chat is absent.

**Motivation** — #2931: the chat never asks which profile `nax plan` / `nax run` use, or which agent and model write and review the spec, and there is no config to set them.

**Approach** — Add `schemas-chat.ts` with `ChatConfigSchema` and `DEFAULT_SPEC_FIX_ROUNDS`, the optional root `chat` key on `NaxConfigSchema`, `runtime-types-chat.ts`, and `chat?` on `NaxConfig`. Export `DEFAULT_SPEC_FIX_ROUNDS` from `src/config/index.ts` and the chat types from `src/config/types.ts` (and the barrel), as `bot` is. `NaxConfigSchema.parse({})` stays unchanged.

Config defaults live in the Zod schema. The new `chat` root key is optional with no root default, so `NaxConfigSchema.parse({})` and `DEFAULT_CONFIG` are unchanged.

- Every field is optional; `specFixRounds` defaults to `DEFAULT_SPEC_FIX_ROUNDS` (2) once `workflow` is present, and is an integer from 1 to 10.
- A profile name must match `^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*$`; a model must match `^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._:/-]*$` (the patterns `job-argv.ts` already applies to launch input). `agent` is a non-empty string.
- The block can be set in global, project or profile config; `nax chat --profile <name>` already overlays a profile, so a profile that sets `chat.workflow` selects these values for the session. Arrays are replaced by a later layer, as `deepMergeConfig` already does.
- An empty `profiles` array means not set.

Step key per launch kind: `plan` -> `plan`; `run` and `finish` -> `run`; `bot_review` -> `botReview`; `bot_fix` -> `botFix`.

Unknown-profile validation for `chat.workflow` profile names happens at launch, not at config load: profile discovery is repo-scoped and async, while config parsing is not. This replaces item 5 of the #2931 design sketch.

**Scope** — In: new chat schema/types, optional NaxConfigSchema/NaxConfig fields, config exports and defaults/layering regressions. Out: applying launch defaults and launch-time profile existence validation (US-006), workflow prompt rendering and provider composition (US-007). Validating `chat.workflow` profile names at config load. Loading a flow profile mid-session from a chat message ("use flow profile X"); a profile is selected with `nax chat --profile`.

**Interface** —
```typescript
// packages/nax/src/config/schemas-chat.ts
export const DEFAULT_SPEC_FIX_ROUNDS = 2;
export const ChatConfigSchema: z.ZodType; // { workflow?: ChatWorkflowConfig }

// packages/nax/src/config/runtime-types-chat.ts
export interface ChatStepConfig { readonly profiles?: readonly string[] }
export interface ChatBotStepConfig extends ChatStepConfig { readonly model?: string }
export interface ChatAgentChoice { readonly agent?: string; readonly model?: string }
export interface ChatWorkflowConfig {
  readonly plan?: ChatStepConfig;
  readonly run?: ChatStepConfig;
  readonly botReview?: ChatBotStepConfig;
  readonly botFix?: ChatBotStepConfig;
  readonly specWriter?: ChatAgentChoice;
  readonly specReviewer?: ChatAgentChoice;
  readonly specFixRounds: number;
}
export interface ChatConfig { readonly workflow?: ChatWorkflowConfig }

// NaxConfig in packages/nax/src/config/runtime-types.ts
chat?: import("./runtime-types-chat").ChatConfig;
```

Configuration example:
```json
{
  "chat": {
    "workflow": {
      "plan": { "profiles": ["codex-plan", "cc-acceptance"] },
      "run": { "profiles": ["codex-review"] },
      "botReview": { "profiles": [], "model": "openai-codex/gpt-6.1-sol" },
      "botFix": { "model": "openai-codex/gpt-6.1-sol" },
      "specWriter": { "agent": "general", "model": "openai-codex/gpt-6.1-sol" },
      "specReviewer": { "agent": "code-reviewer" },
      "specFixRounds": 2
    }
  }
}
```

Failure handling: `chat.workflow` with an invalid profile name, model, agent or `specFixRounds` fails config parse with a Zod issue at that path. No new default root object, config-load profile listing or changes to deepMergeConfig's existing array replacement are needed.

**Goal** — Apply configured profiles and bot models consistently to standalone and chained launches, validate config-supplied profile names and disclose their sources on cards.

**Motivation** — The user sees the choice only on the approval card.

**Approach** — `applyWorkflowDefaults(input, workflow)`, a pure function, in this order:

1. When `input.profiles` is non-empty, profiles are left as passed (the chat request wins).
2. Otherwise, when the step's `profiles` is non-empty, `input.profiles` becomes that list and `profilesFrom` is `chat.workflow.<stepKey>`.
3. For bot kinds only: when `input.model` is undefined and the step's `model` is set, `input.model` becomes it and `modelFrom` is `chat.workflow.<stepKey>`. Rules 1-2 and rule 3 are independent: a launch can take profiles from the request and the model from config.
4. For `run` and `finish`, the existing rule "no profiles -> the PRD's planned profile" applies only when steps 1-2 leave `profiles` empty.

`launchJob` (`jobs-launch.ts`) applies it after `parseJobInput` and before `prepareTarget`. `resolveStepTarget` (`chain-tools.ts`) applies it to each chain step before `buildChainStepArgv`, so a chain card's argv and the step's later launch argv agree.

`workflow-defaults.ts` also exports `unknownWorkflowProfile(applied, repoRoot): Promise<string | undefined>`. When `applied.profilesFrom` is set it lists the known names through its own `_workflowDefaultsDeps.listProfileNames` (global and project names from `listProfiles`, deduplicated and sorted; a leaf dep, so `jobs-launch.ts` takes no value import from `jobs-provider.ts` and no import cycle forms) and returns `[chat] <profilesFrom>.profiles names unknown profile <name>; known: <names joined by ", ">` for the first unknown name, else undefined. `launchJob` runs it after `prepareTarget` (so a missing spec or PRD is reported first) and returns its message as an error before any card. `resolveStepTarget` returns it as `stepError(step, message)` before the chain card. Explicitly passed names keep today's behaviour.

A `run` whose profiles come from `chat.workflow.run` and differ from the PRD's planned profile shows the existing warning `the PRD was planned with profile <planned>, not <profiles>`, as an explicit request does today.

Card lines: when `profilesFrom` is set, the profiles line reads `profiles <names joined by ", "> (<profilesFrom>)`; when `modelFrom` is set, a detail line `model <model> (<modelFrom>)` is added. Unset config leaves today's lines byte-for-byte.

Step key per launch kind: `plan` -> `plan`; `run` and `finish` -> `run`; `bot_review` -> `botReview`; `bot_fix` -> `botFix`. An empty `profiles` array means not set.

`JobsProviderOptions` gains `readonly workflow?: ChatWorkflowConfig`; `createHostJobs` passes `options.config.chat?.workflow`.

`buildJobsInstructions` line 7 target: `Leave profiles empty to use the profile the PRD was planned with, unless chat.workflow sets profiles for that step, which then apply; the approval card names them. Pass profiles only to override, with names from `nax-jobs__list`.` The prefix is kept, so the existing `toContain` assertion still holds.

`PROFILES_SCHEMA.description` target: `Config profiles for this job, applied in order. Omit it to use chat.workflow's profiles for this step when configured, else the profile the PRD was planned with (run) or the default config; pass it only to override. nax-jobs__list returns the available names.`

**Scope** — In: pure defaults/provenance helper and separate profile-list leaf, JobsProviderOptions/workflow threading, standalone preparation/card path, chain step resolution, jobs prompt/schema descriptions and tool-entry seam tests. Out: config schema/types (US-005), workflow instructions and workflow provider config (US-007), changing explicit-profile behavior, checkout/approval protections or command parsing patterns. `finish` has no `chat.workflow` key of its own; it uses `run`. Validating `chat.workflow` profile names at config load.

**Interface** —
```typescript
// packages/nax/src/chat/jobs/workflow-defaults.ts
export interface WorkflowDefaultsApplied<T> {
  readonly input: T;
  /** e.g. "chat.workflow.plan"; present only when profiles came from config. */
  readonly profilesFrom?: string;
  /** e.g. "chat.workflow.botReview"; present only when the model came from config. */
  readonly modelFrom?: string;
}
export function applyWorkflowDefaults<T extends { readonly kind: ChatJobKind; readonly profiles: readonly string[]; readonly model?: string }>(
  input: T,
  workflow: ChatWorkflowConfig | undefined,
): WorkflowDefaultsApplied<T>;
/** Separate from `_jobsProviderDeps.listProfileNames` (which stays for `nax-jobs__list`); same body. */
export const _workflowDefaultsDeps: { listProfileNames: (repoRoot: string) => Promise<readonly string[]> };
export function unknownWorkflowProfile(applied: WorkflowDefaultsApplied<{ readonly profiles: readonly string[] }>, repoRoot: string): Promise<string | undefined>;
```

US-006 consumes `ChatWorkflowConfig` (US-005) through `JobsProviderOptions.workflow`; its seam ACs enter at the `nax-jobs__plan` tool run and the `nax-jobs__chain` tool run. Preserve provenance while preparing the target; defaults must not mutate caller inputs. Keep missing artifact checks ahead of configured-profile existence errors. With no `workflow` option every existing launch, chain and card test sees today's behaviour. This includes run fallback for composite PRD profile a+b and the existing chain approval/admission boundary.
